# Build parallelism (leave 1 core free)

On this machine, when running compilations or builds that support parallel jobs, cap parallelism at **one less than the available CPU count** so the system stays responsive.

## Compute job count

Before a parallel build, derive the job count once and reuse it:

```bash
BUILD_JOBS=$(($(nproc) - 1))
[ "$BUILD_JOBS" -lt 1 ] && BUILD_JOBS=1
```

On this machine (`nproc` = 8), use **7** jobs unless the tool or project already pins a lower limit.

## Apply when possible

Use `BUILD_JOBS` (or the equivalent flag) for tools that support it:

| Tool | Example |
|------|---------|
| **cargo** | `cargo build -j "$BUILD_JOBS"` or `CARGO_BUILD_JOBS="$BUILD_JOBS" cargo build` |
| **make** | `make -j"$BUILD_JOBS"` |
| **ninja** | `ninja -j "$BUILD_JOBS"` |
| **cmake** | `cmake --build . --parallel "$BUILD_JOBS"` |
| **go** | `go build -p "$BUILD_JOBS"` |
| **meson** | `meson compile -C build -j "$BUILD_JOBS"` |
| **npm/pnpm** | Respect project scripts; if setting workers explicitly, use `$BUILD_JOBS` |

## Rules

- Prefer explicit `-j` / `--parallel` flags over unbounded defaults when the tool would otherwise use all cores
- Do not override a project’s documented, intentional job limit (e.g. CI config or `CARGO_BUILD_JOBS` in `.cargo/config.toml`) unless asked to change it
- Single-threaded commands (e.g. one `rustc` invocation, `g++ file.cpp`) need no special handling
- If parallelism is unsupported or unknown, run the build normally — do not block on adding flags

## Examples

```bash
# ❌ BAD — uses all cores, can freeze the desktop
cargo build
make -j$(nproc)

# ✅ GOOD — leaves one core free
BUILD_JOBS=$(($(nproc) - 1)); [ "$BUILD_JOBS" -lt 1 ] && BUILD_JOBS=1
cargo build -j "$BUILD_JOBS"
make -j"$BUILD_JOBS"
cmake --build build --parallel "$BUILD_JOBS"
```
